  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "Ramadan is the holiest month in the Islamic calendar, a period of fasting, prayer, and reflection where Muslims abstain from food, drink, smoking, and negative behaviors from dawn to sunset. It marks when the Quran was first revealed to Prophet Muhammad, aiming to foster self-control, gratitude, and closer devotion to God. Key Rules and Practices Fasting: Healthy adult Muslims fast daily from dawn (Fajr) to sunset (Maghrib). Exceptions: Exemptions apply to children, the elderly, the sick, pregnant/breastfeeding individuals, and travelers. Daily Routine: Meals are eaten before dawn (suhoor) and after sunset (iftar). Spiritual Focus: Increased prayer, reading the Quran, charity, and avoiding bad habits like gossip or fighting. Special Prayers: Tarawih prayers are held at night in mosques. Significance and Purpose God Consciousness: The main goal is to develop taqwa (God-consciousness or spiritual discipline). Empathy: Fasting helps Muslims empathize with the poor and less fortunate. Reflection: It is a time for self-reflection, seeking forgiveness, and strengthening community ties. Conclusion: The month ends with the celebration of Eid al-Fitr. Health Benefits Detoxification: Abstinence from food/drink can allow the body to detoxify. Self-Control: Promotes breaking bad habits and improving dietary habits. Mental Health: Offers a time for spiritual healing, increased calm, and improved humility.",
